#!/usr/bin/env bash

# The devenv environment, loaded by direnv on directory entry.
#
# **Why direnv and not `devenv shell`.** `devenv shell` enters a *subshell*, so
# the environment ends with it. direnv modifies the shell in place, which means
# an editor, a REPL and the agent harness all inherit the toolchain rather than
# each having to be started inside `devenv shell`. The hook is already in
# ~/.zshrc; this file is the half that was missing.
#
# **`eval "$(devenv direnvrc)"` defines `use_devenv`, and `use devenv` calls
# it.** The eval takes the loader from the devenv *on PATH* rather than from
# ~/.config/direnv/direnvrc, so upgrading devenv upgrades the integration with
# it and there is no second copy to fall out of date. Only then is `use devenv`
# a command that exists. Re-defining a function the global direnvrc already
# defined is harmless.
#
# **`eval` rather than `source_url`.** The pinned-URL form would fix the loader
# to one revision, so every project would need its own manual bump when devenv
# ships a fix. Tracked at https://devenv.sh/integrations/direnv/.
#
# devenv.nix does the rest: erlang, just, jujutsu, git, erlfmt, gh, and `elp` as
# the Erlang language server. `.direnv/` and `.devenv/` are already in
# .gitignore; this file is committed on purpose -- it is how every other checkout
# gets the same toolchain.
#
# **Committed on purpose, and that is a commitment this file used to break.** It
# was written here and then left untracked, so the checkout that had it was the
# only one that worked. A file whose purpose is "every other checkout gets this"
# has to be in the repository, and the README's `direnv allow` step is the other
# half of the same promise.
#
# **`devenv` must be on PATH before this runs**, because line 2 calls it. It is
# not fetched from here -- install it once per machine
# (`nix profile install nixpkgs#devenv`), which is the same step CI takes and for
# the same reason: this file loads an environment, it does not build one.
#
# **Editing this file re-blocks every open shell**, and the symptom is a toolchain
# that has silently gone missing rather than an error. direnv trusts a revision of
# this file, not the path, so `direnv allow` has to be re-run afterwards. That is
# the correct behaviour and the reason it is not worked around here: the whole
# point of the prompt is that this file runs shell code, so an edit to it is
# exactly the event that should require a human to look.

eval "$(devenv direnvrc)"

use devenv